lisa-design:
- Round 3 (design §13.4). R78 continuity policy_id is the LITERAL `<plugin-path>/<file>` (continuityPolicyId), never path.Join (R77 correction; api f50e4d8 SeedPath cleans the id for the file seed and trims the raw id for the path seed). `plugin-path` comes from the report entry whose `source` is the bundle's `extends` (pluginPathFor over reportSets, placeholder instance first). When no instance reports it (older agent, or no plugin loads the source directly any more) Override inserts nothing and the editor shows "evidence for this override will start a new stream". A vendor module that already declares policy_id is kept as is.
- Override-from-template (no vendor source) uses the continuity id when the plugin path is known, else no policy_id (same rule as the pre-fill); Add file / new bundle use `<bundle>/<file>` (newModulePolicyId). Template violations use the object form `violation[{...}] if { … }` (every plugin lib evaluates it); the client contract hints therefore only flag `violation[k] := v` (api checkViolationRule), not the object form.
- utils/agent-config/policy-identity.ts ports policyeval.SeedPath + bundleRelative + Go path.Clean so the UI judges stream continuity exactly like the API/agent: an override continues when its (policy_file, _policy_path) seed equals the vendor module's. The bundle's own plugin path, when unreported, is replaced by a stand-in (`/inline/<b>/current/bundle`); it only matters for ids equal to the bundle's own location. Vendor sources of overridden modules of the SELECTED bundle are read once (cached) so a vendor policy_id is compared; other bundles use what is cached.
- R78 editor warnings are client ContractHints with the agent's codes (policy-stream-forked, policy-package-changed), warning severity; an API/agent problem with the same code and no row suppresses them for that file (dedupe). Stream labels are shown only for authored policy modules (overridden/added/set); inherited vendor modules get one bundle-level note (they fork to path-based streams when the bundle replaces the source in a plugin, since plugins load them from the inline path) instead of per-file fetches.
- R79/R80: the UI relies ONLY on each instance's reported `plugins[].inline-policies` (no client version comparison); MIN_INLINE_POLICY_LIB = v0.8.0 is used only in message text. Gate (utils/agent-config/plugin-compat.ts inlineGateIssues) mirrors agent cmd/compat.go: per validation-set instance (validationInstanceIds, like the API), per enabled plugin of merge(base, draft overlay) that loads an existing inline bundle; blocking when support=unsupported AND the overlay touches /plugins/<p>/source, gives an inline entry the file lacks, or touches /policy_bundles/<b> of a used bundle; otherwise a non-blocking warning; unknown always warns. A plugin's own report counts while the draft's effective source equals the reported source; a changed source is judged by another plugin of the instance running that build, else unknown (no issue). The issues are fed into draft.clientIssues via useOverlayDraft `extraIssues`, so they block Review & save, the live preview and show in the pending bar like other client issues.
- R79 action gating: Assign / Create-bundle rows disable assigning to an unsupported plugin (unassigning stays possible); Override and Add file are disabled for a bundle used by an unsupported plugin; the Effective view's policy-list editor refuses an inline: entry for it. Texts are dynamic and live with the logic (plugin-compat.ts) / constants.ts, like the rest of this PR, not src/config/tooltips.ts.
